In `wp_get_shortlink()` with the `query` context, `$post_id` is taken from `get_queried_object_id()` while `$post` is taken from `get_post( $post_id )`. When the queried object ID is non-zero but `get_post()` returns `null`, the `! empty( $post_id )` branch still runs and reads `$post->post_type`:

{{{
E_WARNING: Attempt to read property "post_type" on null in wp-includes/link-template.php
}}}

This is reached through `wp_shortlink_header()` on `template_redirect` (and `wp_shortlink_wp_head()`).

We saw this on WordPress.org from a scanner that POSTed a large number of junk parameters to a handbook landing page (a singular page whose query is adjusted in `pre_get_posts`). In that case `is_singular()` is true and the queried object ID is set, but no post is found for it.

The `post` context branch already guards against this by only setting `$post_id` from `$post->ID`. The proposed fix does the same for the `query` context:

{{{#!php
<?php
	if ( 'query' === $context && is_singular() ) {
		$post = get_post( get_queried_object_id() );
		if ( $post ) {
			$post_id = $post->ID;
		}
	}
}}}

PR: https://github.com/WordPress/wordpress-develop/pull/14543

Related: #51376 (a different notice in the same function).

When you register a metadata using `register_meta()` function, or one of its variants, such as `register_post_meta()`, the metadata is present in the response of `get_registered_meta_keys()` only in the first test of a file...

I found this issue writing tests for a plugin, in a structure created based on the `wp scaffold plugin-tests` cli command.

= How to reproduce = 

Write a plugin with jus this simple code:

{{{#!php
<?php


add_action('init', 'test_register_post_meta');
function test_register_post_meta() {
	register_post_meta('post', 'test_meta', [
		'show_in_rest' => true,
		'single' => true,
		'type' => 'number',
		'description' => 'test metadata'
	]);
}
}}}

And then run these simple tests:

{{{#!php
<?php

class TestRegisterPostMeta extends \WP_UnitTestCase {

	function test_register_post_meta() {
		
		$registered_meta = get_registered_meta_keys('post', 'post');
		$this->assertArrayHasKey('test_meta', $registered_meta); // it passes!
		
	}
	
	function test_register_post_meta_again() {
		
		$registered_meta = get_registered_meta_keys('post', 'post');
		$this->assertArrayHasKey('test_meta', $registered_meta); // it fails! 
		
	}
	
	
}
}}}

This is very odd, but the second test, which is identical to the first, fails.

(This was initially reported privately as a security issue but it was considered not a core vulnerability.
I still think it is worth hardening so I am reporting it as a bug.)

wp-admin/js/post.js passes the value of the href attribute to {{{$()}}} without sanitizing it.
This is dangerous because {{{$()}}} accepts both a selector or raw HTML.
In theory, this could be exploited if an attacker can inject basic HTML on the post edit page. (But core does not provide a way to exploit this, it would need to be chained with an HTML injection vulnerability).

For example this payload would trigger the problem if it can be inserted on /wp-admin/post-new.php:
{{{
<div class="categorydiv" id="taxonomy-foo"></div>
<div id="foo-tabs"><a href="&lt;img src=x onerror=alert()&gt;">Click me!</a></div>
}}}

Using document.querySelectorAll() would ensure the input is always interpreted as a selector:
{{{
$(document.querySelectorAll(t)).show();
}}}

The `serialize_block_attributes()` function encodes the attributes array as-is, so an array that PHP sees as a list is serialized as a JSON array rather than an object. The block delimiter grammar only accepts an object, so a block whose attributes come out this way is no longer a block when its content is parsed again.

This happens when a block's attribute names are numeric. The `parse_blocks()` function decodes the delimiter's JSON into associative arrays, and PHP turns numeric-string keys into integer keys, so `{"0":"a"}` decodes to `array( 0 => 'a' )`, which `wp_json_encode()` then encodes as `["a"]`:

{{{#!php
<?php
$serialized = serialize_blocks( parse_blocks( '<!-- wp:paragraph {"0":"a"} /-->' ) );
// <!-- wp:paragraph ["a"] /-->

$reparsed = parse_blocks( $serialized );
// $reparsed[0]['blockName'] is null: the paragraph is now freeform content.
}}}

The same happens for `{"0":"a","1":"b"}`, which becomes `["a","b"]`. Numeric names that do not count up from zero, such as `{"1":"a"}`, are not affected, since PHP does not see them as a list.

Called directly, the function also returns:

 * `[]` for an empty array, rather than `{}`. Core's callers check `empty()` first, so they are not affected.
 * An empty string when the attributes cannot be encoded, such as when a value is `INF`: `wp_json_encode()` returns `false`, which `strtr()` turns into `''`. That is not JSON, and the JavaScript `serializeAttributes()` never returns it. The block grammar happens to tolerate it, since core's callers surround the attributes with spaces, so a paragraph with `'align' => 'wide'` and `'ratio' => INF` is serialized as `<!-- wp:paragraph  /-->`. It is still parsed as a paragraph, but with none of its attributes, not even `align`.

Besides a round trip through `parse_blocks()` and `serialize_blocks()`, block delimiters are re-serialized through this function by `wp_strip_custom_css_from_blocks()`, and by the block delimiter normalization in `wp_sanitize_html_kses()`, which `wp_kses()` uses when the `wp_kses_force_legacy_parser` filter, new in 7.2, returns false.

The docblock of `serialize_block_attributes()` says its output must remain in sync with the JavaScript `serializeAttributes()` in the block editor. That function takes an attributes object (`Record< string, unknown >`), which `JSON.stringify()` always encodes as `{…}`.

Nested attribute values have a similar problem, which is out of scope here: since the delimiter's JSON is decoded into associative arrays, a nested `{}` or `{"0":"x"}` is re-serialized as `[]` or `["x"]`.

Related: #65817

🤖 ''Ticket drafted with support from Claude Opus 5.5.''

When the Media Library grid uses the "Load more" button, it shows how many media items are loaded:

{{{#!js
this.loadMoreCount.$el.text(
	/* translators: 1: Number of displayed attachments, 2: Number of total attachments. */
	sprintf(
		__( 'Showing %1$s of %2$s media items' ),
		this.collection.length,
		this.collection.getTotalAttachments()
	)
);
}}}

This is `updateLoadMoreView()` in `src/js/media/views/attachments/browser.js` (line 611 on trunk), added in [50829] for #50105.

There are two problems:

 1. The string has no plural form, so a library with one media item shows "Showing 1 of 1 media items", and translators get one form for every count.
 2. The translators comment sits above `sprintf()` instead of the `__()` call, so it is not extracted. `wp i18n make-pot` on trunk's `browser.js` gives the string without a `#.` line, and the wp/dev Hindi export from translate.wordpress.org has no comment for it either.

Since [62632] (#65564), infinite scrolling is on by default, so this count only shows for users who turn it off in their profile or on sites that filter `media_library_infinite_scrolling` to false.

=== Steps to reproduce ===

 1. On a site with exactly one media item, go to Users > Profile, check "Disable infinite scrolling in the Media Library grid view" and save.
 2. Go to Media > Library in grid view.
 3. Below the item, the count reads "Showing 1 of 1 media items".

Tested on WordPress 7.1.3, whose `updateLoadMoreView()` is identical to trunk.

=== Suggested fix ===

Use `_n()`, with the plural form following the total, and put the comment directly above the `_n()` call:

{{{#!js
this.loadMoreCount.$el.text(
	sprintf(
		/* translators: 1: Number of displayed attachments, 2: Number of total attachments. */
		_n(
			'Showing %1$s of %2$s media item',
			'Showing %1$s of %2$s media items',
			this.collection.getTotalAttachments()
		),
		this.collection.length,
		this.collection.getTotalAttachments()
	)
);
}}}

With this, `make-pot` extracts the comment and both forms, from the source file and from the built `wp-includes/js/media-views.js`. Existing translations of the old string do not carry over to the new pair.

Related: the open count tickets (#57488, #58729, #65513) are about the numbers, not the wording. [https://github.com/WordPress/wordpress-develop/pull/12392 PR 12392] for #65513 adds an e2e assertion for "Showing 1 of 1 media items", which this change would turn into "Showing 1 of 1 media item".

